  "id": "ex-12-modalverben-b2",
  "intro": "Standalone drill set, not tied to any lesson. Beyond their everyday (objektiv) meanings, the modal verbs have a second, subjektiv use for guesses, hearsay, and claims — same modal, no object case involved, just a different degree of certainty: müssen = a near-certain inference (Er muss zu Hause sein — the light is on), dürfte = a cautious assumption, können/könnte = a bare possibility, sollen = hearsay (\"people say\" — Er soll reich sein), wollen = a claim the speaker doubts (Er will das nicht gewusst haben), mögen = a concession (Er mag Recht haben). To talk about the past, the main verb becomes Infinitiv II (Partizip II + haben/sein): Er muss krank gewesen sein. Blocks: A Basistraining → B Vertiefung → C Sprachbausteine → D Wiederholung. Answers in solutions.md.\n",
  "lesson": "EX/12-modalverben-b2",
  "level": "B2",
